menu "downlink"

config DL_DEVICE_ID
    string "Device id"
    default "downlink"
    help
        DHCP hostname, the portal AP's suffix ("downlink-<id>") and the MQTT
        topic segment (downlink/<id>/...). Give each board its own.
        Overridable from the portal; NVS wins over this.

config DL_BOARD
    string "Board name"
    default "n16r8"
    help
        Reported in the MQTT status, and how OTA will pick the right image
        for this board.

config DL_STREAM_URL
    string "Stream URL"
    default "http://icecast.example:8000/stream"
    help
        Icecast mount serving Ogg Opus (RUMP with codec = Opus). A seed:
        copied to NVS on first boot (unless it is the placeholder), then
        changed by the portal or the MQTT "url" command.

menu "I2S (PCM5102A)"

config DL_I2S_BCK_GPIO
    int "BCK GPIO"
    default 4
    range 0 48

config DL_I2S_WS_GPIO
    int "LRCK / WS GPIO"
    default 5
    range 0 48

config DL_I2S_DOUT_GPIO
    int "DIN (data out) GPIO"
    default 6
    range 0 48

endmenu

config DL_VOLUME
    int "Volume (0-100)"
    default 70
    range 0 100
    help
        Digital gain before the DAC. 100 is unity; each step below is
        0.5 dB, so 70 is -15 dB and 0 is silence. The PCM5102A has no
        hardware volume. A default: the MQTT "volume" command changes it
        live and saves it to NVS, which wins from then on.

config DL_RING_KB
    int "Stream ring buffer (KB, PSRAM)"
    default 512
    range 64 4096
    help
        Compressed bytes between the network and the decoder. 512 KB is
        about 30 s of 128 kbps Opus: it absorbs Icecast's connect burst
        and rides out a short WiFi drop.

config DL_PREBUFFER_MS
    int "Prebuffer before playback (ms of audio)"
    default 2000
    range 250 30000
    help
        Playback starts (and restarts after an underrun) once this much
        audio is buffered. Measured in audio from the Ogg granule
        positions, not bytes: Opus is variable-bitrate, and a quiet passage
        is ~40x smaller than a loud one.

config DL_BUFFER_MS
    int "Buffer depth to hold (ms of audio)"
    default 4000
    range 500 60000
    help
        The depth the drift correction holds against the difference between
        the source's clock and this board's. Icecast's connect burst usually
        lands near this on its own; deeper rides out longer network
        stalls, shallower is closer to live.

config DL_BUFFER_MAX_MS
    int "Catch up above (ms of audio)"
    default 12000
    range 2000 300000
    help
        Above this depth the player decodes and discards until it is back
        at DL_BUFFER_MS. Joining an Icecast mount during a silent passage
        can hand over minutes of audio in one byte-sized burst.

menu "Announcements"

config DL_ANNOUNCE_QUEUE
    int "Announcements queued at once (fetching, ready and playing)"
    default 4
    range 1 16

config DL_ANNOUNCE_MAX_KB
    int "Largest clip (KB)"
    default 512
    range 16 4096
    help
        A larger clip fails with "too large". At 48 kbps, 512 KB is about
        85 s. Must not exceed the budget below.

config DL_ANNOUNCE_BUDGET_KB
    int "PSRAM for queued clips (KB)"
    default 2048
    range 64 8192
    help
        All downloaded clips together. A fetch that would exceed it waits
        for a clip ahead to finish. The SuperMini (2 MB PSRAM) uses 768.

config DL_DUCK_ATTACK_MS
    int "Duck: music down over (ms)"
    default 300
    range 10 5000

config DL_DUCK_TAIL_MS
    int "Duck: hold after the clip (ms)"
    default 150
    range 0 5000

config DL_DUCK_RELEASE_MS
    int "Duck: music back up over (ms)"
    default 700
    range 10 10000

endmenu

config DL_STALL_S
    int "Reconnect when no stream data for (s)"
    default 10
    range 3 120

config DL_MAX_BACKOFF_S
    int "Stream reconnect backoff ceiling (s)"
    default 30
    range 2 600

endmenu
